1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Hey, I hope you had a chance to see a lot of wild animals while you were at summer camp this summer.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
I know I did

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Come to think of it. most of them were wearing a scout uniform with my troop cover on it.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It is podcast number 84..

6
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Welcome back to the scout master podcast.

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
This is Clark Green.

8
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
So chess merit badge, new merit badge.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
That doesn't happen very often, by the way, that we have a new merit badge

10
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:15,000
And now we have one that involves the game of chess, which you know, at first blush doesn't seem to have a whole lot of connection there. right,

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
There's not a lot of connection between chess and scouting and there's not a lot of connection between a lot of merit badge subjects and the core elements of scouting.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
But this is what I really like about the chess merit badge is that it has some connection to some of the core subjects.

13
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:42,000
People can scoff at it for being boring or dull, but you know there wouldn't be any video games or games of much level of a strategic sophistication if we didn't have chess to begin with.

14
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And the history of chess is actually kind of interesting because it's the history of games:

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Baseball, football, basketball- just about any team sport bears some similarities to chess.

16
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
You have players with specific roles and proscribed ways they can move and act on the field.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Some have really pivotal roles,

18
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Some are in more supporting roles.

19
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Chess is a martial game.

20
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:07,000
I mean it's about conquering the other side and everything.

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:21,000
So some of the connotations may not transfer comfortably, but the strategy, the ability to think ahead of chess, to changing situations and to execute a plan, all lend themselves well to the study of leadership

22
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
And that's one of the things that we work within scouting.

23
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Now, admittedly, chess isn't a physically demanding activity,

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
It's not out-oriented, but it is great fun

25
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:36,000
And I know a chess board goes along on most of our camping trips and you know it's taking care of some rainy afternoons. before.

26
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Chad Fisher wrote in about chess merit badge and he said:

27
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
my son is on the chess team at school and he's very excited about this merit badge.

28
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Hey, you know, I'm sure that there are many scouts out there who are really anxious to take a look at it and to earn the merit badge.

29
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
Chad is a varsity team coach.

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Now if you haven't been around for very long you might not know about varsity teams.

31
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
It is a part of the scouting program.

32
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,000
In some places it's very popular.

33
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
In some places it's almost unknown.

34
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:14,000
But Chad is a varsity team coach and his blog is varsityteamvenueblogspotcom.

35
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,000
I just discovered it this week and I recommend you get over there.

36
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
I'll have a link to Chad's blog in the post that contains this podcast.

37
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:24,000
More about chess:

38
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:32,000
Jason Pettis said my troop always brings two or three boards along the summer camp and the scouts use some of their downtime challenging each other and the adults,

39
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
as well as starting teaching the game to the scouts that don't know it.

40
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Sometimes staff members come and play a game or two.

41
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Walter Underwood wrote in and said a chess set is a great way to keep boys scouts busy and out of trouble during those spare chunks of time at summer camp.

42
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,000
It really worked:

43
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Two players and a swarm of kibitzers.

44
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
One chess game could keep ten scouts busy.

45
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:00,000
No doubt

46
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,000
I'm excited.

47
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:08,000
I'm looking forward to getting the chess merit badge book and I'm going to become a counselor for chess merit badge.

48
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:19,000
I learned to play when I was in second grade- and there's a little story about that on the blog, if you're interested- and went on to be on the chess team in high school and everything.

49
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Play as much as I used to,

50
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,000
But I think I can probably brush up on my technique and become a merit badge counselor for that particular merit badge.

51
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,000
Let's see. moving on,

52
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
We're looking at who wrote in this week.

53
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Frank Maynardt had this comment about podcast 81.

54
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,000
In podcast 81, we were dealing with scouting and discipline

55
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
And Frank said: I have a list of attributes of a boss versus a leader

56
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
And one of them is a boss fixes blame and a leader fixes the problem.

57
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Well, thanks for that, Frank,

58
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:59,000
And that points at the idea of discipline, and scouting is not about fixing blame as much as it is fixing the problem most of the time.

59
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Frank went on to say: I don't know if I agree with your process of having a scout who has run into disciplinary trouble appear before the troop committee

60
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,000
When it comes to deciding his future course with the troop. it has the feeling of putting the scout on trial.

61
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Unless his behavior poses a clear and present danger, the situation should be disclosed to the committee.

62
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
But I would really rather the scout master handle it, consulting with the committee chair as necessary to make him aware of the situation and discuss possible solutions.

63
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
I agree with you, Frank, more than you think.

64
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:35,000
I think what we were discussing in podcast 81, when I particularly referred to this process, in other words of the scout going and talking to the troop committee.

65
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:46,000
That was in a case where there was pretty much going to be a clear and present danger to safety and a propriety as far as the troop is involved.

66
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Had this scout not, you know, adjusted his attitude and actions.

67
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Should a scout master handle it individually? boy, that's a tough judgment call.

68
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
In this particular case, the actions of this scout came to the tension of the civil authorities.

69
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
In other words, he got in trouble with the police

70
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:11,000
And it was over something that was kind of kind of worrisome

71
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:19,000
And the way that and it ended up being not a troop related scouting event, but it was scouting related.

72
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:29,000
I did discuss this with the committee chairman and what we came up with is that this was not going to be a decision on whether the scout was going to be able to go forward or not with our troop.

73
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
It shouldn't be my decision, shouldn't be his decision.

74
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:42,000
It needs to be a group decision. because if we decided that he was going to move forward with the troop, you know we were going to have to defend that decision to parents who might be concerned about his actions,

75
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:55,000
And so we would do that much better if there was a more general understanding and if it was a decision on the part of the whole troop rather than just me or the committee chair.

76
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
So that's why we took that particular action.

77
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,000
I do agree with you, Frank, in most cases that's not going to be necessary,

78
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:08,000
But in this particular one we felt that this was the best way forward

79
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:19,000
And you know, progress report on that. the scout was told that for him to go on and scouting with our troop, that's what he was going to need to do. so far,

80
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
No reply, unfortunately.

81
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:30,000
But I'm going to need to leave that right where it is for now and we're going to move on because it's a pretty big podcast.

82
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:31,000
We've got a lot of things to talk about

83
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:38,000
In Scout Mastership. in seven minutes or less, we're going to talk about a recent troop leadership summit that we held,

84
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:46,000
And I just want to give you a few ideas, tell you what we did, and maybe you'll be able to take some things away from that.

85
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:56,000
The Great Brotherhood of Service is worldwide, scouting is worldwide, and I came across an interesting article from Scouts Australia

86
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:58,000
And I thought that would be fun to share with you.

87
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
And finally, we're going to wind it up with a Scout Masters Minute.

88
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
I think that's enough for any podcast, don't you?

89
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Well, let's get started, shall we?

90
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:11,000
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

91
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
Scout troops have to plan.

92
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
We plan with the Patrol Leaders Council.

93
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:29,000
You know, I think we all go through our several different planning processes in a little bit different ways for each individual troop,

94
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
But there are some common threads that run through each one of them.

95
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Larry Geiger- oh gee, I guess it's a couple of months ago now- had an article on scoutmastercgcom where he talked about the planning process

96
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:49,000
that he uses, just some little logistics and the philosophy behind it,

97
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
And that was a good post

98
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
And it got me thinking about the planning process we use.

99
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:02,000
And so, in consultation with our troop committee chair, we set up our first annual troop leadership summit.

100
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Sounds important, doesn't it?

101
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:09,000
What it was is a general meeting of all the people with a leadership stake in our troop,

102
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:14,000
So that's the troop committee, myself and the Assistant Scout Masters and the Patrol Leaders Council.

103
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:18,000
We set this up to run over the better part of one Saturday

104
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And I'll tell you what our schedule was and what we did in that schedule,

105
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:28,000
And maybe this will help you refine or come up with a planning process of your own.

106
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:33,000
We started out by meeting at 9 o'clock on Saturday morning

107
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,000
And at that meeting was myself and the troop committee chair and the Patrol Leaders Council.

108
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:44,000
Now the Patrol Leaders Council consists of senior patrol leader, assistant senior patrol leader,

109
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:48,000
the patrol leaders, all the elected patrol leaders in the troop,

110
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:53,000
And we also include the quartermaster and the scribe in on those meetings

111
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
And the junior assistant scout masters are typically there,

112
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
So they were invited to attend as well.

113
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
I read something over at Ask Andy this past week.

114
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:05,000
I don't remember exactly where it was,

115
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:10,000
But the takeaway from it was he pointed out that there's a difference between a leadership position

116
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and a position of responsibility,

117
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:18,000
And he kind of used that differentiation to draw a line at who sits in on Patrol Leaders Council meetings

118
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,000
and who doesn't.

119
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
If you look at the Scout Handbook, you'll see positions of responsibility listed

120
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:27,000
and you'll be able to differentiate them from leadership positions-

121
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
And I'm talking about quartermaster, scribe, librarian, webmaster, et cetera-

122
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
as being positions of responsibility.

123
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,000
I don't have a strong opinion on whether they should be included as a part of a Patrol Leaders Council or not.

124
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:45,000
I don't think that there's too much in the way of officialdom that would point us in one direction or another.

125
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:50,000
But just to let you know that's the way that we have operated-

126
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Made a decision this year that the junior assistant scout masters- we have three of them-

127
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:02,000
would not be official, you know, like participants quite so actively in the Patrol Leaders Council,

128
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:08,000
And here's why We have a very good senior patrol leader and assistant senior patrol leader.

129
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
We've got some pretty sharp patrol leaders.

130
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:16,000
But what we were fighting is that in those parts of the Patrol Leaders Council meetings that I looked in on,

131
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:23,000
it seemed like the junior assistant scout masters had a tendency to kind of monopolize the conversation

132
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
And because they were older and more experienced they had a lot of the answers

133
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:31,000
And so the patrol leaders were kind of sitting and listening rather than participating

134
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:34,000
in the way that probably would have been most productive.

135
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:41,000
So this year we've begun by taking the junior assistant scout masters out of that continuous active role

136
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:47,000
And we've put them into the role that the adult leaders take, slightly modified

137
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,000
in that they're going to be coaches, counselors and mentors

138
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
as far as the Patrol Leaders Council is involved,

139
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
But they're no longer going to sit around the table.

140
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
Quite literally, we have a table, The Patrol Leaders Council sits at it.

141
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
They're going to sit outside of that circle.

142
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
They're going to be available to help,

143
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
But the rule of thumb is they're not going to speak unless they're spoken to.

144
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:14,000
That worked very well because the first session that we had on that Saturday morning,

145
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:18,000
the Patrol Leaders Council sat down and worked on their calendar

146
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:23,000
for the year 2011-2012 scouting activities,

147
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:27,000
And I took the junior assistant scout masters aside with the troop committee chair

148
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and we discussed some other things.

149
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
That turned out to be very useful,

150
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,000
And the Patrol Leaders Council did an excellent job of coming up with a plan.

151
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
So back to the schedule here.

152
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:42,000
Once the Patrol Leaders Council was done and they had their working calendar set up,

153
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:45,000
I took a short period of time to meet with them

154
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:52,000
and to outline what we are going to do as far as troop leadership training.

155
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:56,000
Now, I'm not going to get into a whole lot of detail here,

156
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:02,000
but I'm sure that we'll unroll this as time goes by on the blog and here on the podcast.

157
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:07,000
I'm going to adapt and adopt an old Green Bar Bill practice

158
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:12,000
of using the Green Bar Patrol as a leadership training tool.

159
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Very basically, what this is is that the Patrol Leaders Council becomes a patrol

160
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
for the three or four meetings that are necessary for this training

161
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and the scout master becomes the patrol leader.

162
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:27,000
What we're going to do is we're going to kind of take them through the model evolution

163
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:30,000
of putting a patrol camp out or event together,

164
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:33,000
have some patrol meetings, kind of show them what those pieces are

165
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
and they will be able to experience what a patrol meeting is.

166
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
Hopefully that will help inform their leadership.

167
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
So it's a very hands-on method of training.

168
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
As I adapt that, I will let you know how things go.

169
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
After we did that, what we did is we started reviewing the working calendar

170
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
that the Patrol Leaders Council had put together

171
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
and we just went week by week, meeting by meeting,

172
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
and the senior patrol leader described exactly what their plans were.

173
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
We wrote it out on a big whiteboard

174
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:08,000
and as we went along we identified different tasks

175
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
and different things that would need to be facilitated

176
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
by some of the adult help in the troop,

177
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:16,000
either the troop committee or the assistant scout masters.

178
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:19,000
At this point, for lunch the troop committee

179
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
and the assistant scout masters all showed up,

180
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
so we had all of our adult leadership there

181
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
and they joined the scouts for lunch

182
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
and then after lunch we continued on

183
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:32,000
and we reviewed the calendar.

184
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:35,000
As I said, this review was kind of week by week and everything

185
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
that we were doing is we were teasing out tasks

186
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
that would need to be supported by adults,

187
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:43,000
such as securing campsites and taking care of reservations,

188
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
doing tour permits and all that business.

189
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,000
So we did that and as we went along

190
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
we got adults to commit to the different aspects of things.

191
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:56,000
We've operated this way for quite some years.

192
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
We have people on the troop committee

193
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
who are in the membership and advancement committees,

194
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
subcommittees and things like that,

195
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
and what we have found to be very effective

196
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:10,000
is that sometimes these role-based responsibilities

197
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
don't work quite so well as getting folks

198
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,000
who are interested and engaged

199
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
to just take on a piece of the program

200
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
in support of what the boys have planned,

201
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
and that seems to work better.

202
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
So instead of having, like, a camping committee

203
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:32,000
with somebody who routinely makes reservations

204
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:35,000
for campsites and tour permits and things like that,

205
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
we let that rotate amongst people

206
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
who are willing to take it on for that particular month.

207
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
That's another idea.

208
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
It's certainly not a direction on how things should

209
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
or shouldn't be done,

210
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
but hopefully it'll give you some pause for thought. there

211
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
We got through the calendar

212
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and we ended up all the way through the spring

213
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:55,000
with commitments on making things happen.

214
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
The Patrol Leaders Council put together

215
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
a really interesting program for the year.

216
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
I don't have it sitting in front of me.

217
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
One of the things they did which I thought was interesting:

218
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
we usually meet on Monday nights.

219
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Martin Luther King Day is celebrated on a Monday.

220
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,000
What they came up with this year

221
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:14,000
is they wanted to have somebody come to the troop meeting

222
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and speak about Dr King.

223
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
I thought that was just a wonderful idea,

224
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
something I'd never made the connection with for

225
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:23,000
and since they have off of school that day,

226
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
want to also join in

227
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
and one of the community calls to service

228
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
that we have on Martin Luther King Day.

229
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,000
That's one of several really great ideas

230
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
that they had at the time.

231
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:39,000
Just to sell a button on this, we did our calendar review.

232
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
That started about one o'clock

233
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
and that took about 45 minutes or an hour.

234
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:47,000
Then we had a little bit of a breakout session

235
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
where the troop committee went in and discussed things

236
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
that they needed to think about:

237
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Assistant scout masters. we sat and talked for a while,

238
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
The Patrol Leaders Council was done for the day,

239
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
They did a work on troop equipment and then they had it home

240
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
By three o'clock, we were all done.

241
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:05,000
We started at 9 am, we were out at 3 pm.

242
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
Things moved along at a pretty fair clip.

243
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:11,000
We kept everybody focused

244
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,000
and we didn't digress very much during those meetings

245
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
and that really helped.

246
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
That's basically just an overall outline

247
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
and some of the things that I was particularly interested in

248
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
about our troop leader summit,

249
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
and hopefully that'll maybe spur a few ideas,

250
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
and I would love to hear how you handle planning in your troop

251
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:35,000
and how you put things like this together.

252
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
Do you have an annual meeting like this?

253
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
This is the first time we had everybody

254
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:42,000
with a stake in leadership in the same room at the same time.

255
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
It sounds odd, doesn't it?

256
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:47,000
You would think that that would be a very smart thing to do,

257
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
but we usually have meetings on different days,

258
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
different times, different nights.

259
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Having everybody at the same place at the same time-

260
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
refreshing. it was effective

261
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
and I believe we're going to try it again

262
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
at least once or twice a year.

263
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Hope that helps folks.

264
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Green Hand, Red Ribbon.

265
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:42,000
The scout movement is forming a personal tie

266
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
between the different foreign countries.

267
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:48,000
A living force, a great brotherhood of service,

268
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
a joyous work.

269
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Green Hand, Red Ribbon.

270
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
Hey, from the land down under.

271
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
That's right from Scouts Australia.

272
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
I find an interesting article.

273
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
I thought it had a pretty good slant to it.

274
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Some of the information is stuff that we all share,

275
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
So this should indicate a couple of things, right.

276
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:33,000
First of all, scouting is basically the same

277
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
in most of the world.

278
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:38,000
The scout movement has basically the same thing

279
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
going on in most of the world,

280
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
And you know what

281
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
The concerns and difficulties and challenges

282
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:47,000
are of scout leaders in Australia,

283
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
about the same as us.

284
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:53,000
You know, boys are about the same,

285
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
because we will be talking about boy scouting

286
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:56,000
here in Australia.

287
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,000
People are about the same

288
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
and families are about the same

289
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
and their concerns are going to be basically

290
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,000
our concerns.

291
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
And what I came across was this

292
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:08,000
interesting article on how to kill a scout troop.

293
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
That's right, you heard me right.

294
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
This is how to kill a scout troop,

295
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
And I'm just going to go ahead

296
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
and read some sections of it,

297
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
And we'll get it on the blog pretty soon here too.

298
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,000
So why do we have a story on how to do away with a scout troop?

299
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
Well, it's pretty simple, actually,

300
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:26,000
to help you avoid some of the more common program pitfalls

301
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:29,000
that cause scouts to leave for other activities.

302
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
See, same problem.

303
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:32,000
We have that problem here,

304
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:33,000
They have that problem there.

305
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
You can get away with having

306
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
one or two of these pitfalls,

307
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
but more than that,

308
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
and you're probably leaking far more scouts away

309
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
from the movement than you should be.

310
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Number one: we play the same game every week.

311
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
Now, games are fun,

312
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:49,000
And so they should be.

313
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:52,000
However, not everybody likes the same games

314
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
And there will always be at least one or two scouts

315
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
who don't really fancy the one you're playing that night.

316
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:59,000
They'll be the first to be wishing they were elsewhere

317
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:02,000
if the same game is played. meaning after meaning-

318
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:05,000
Variety is a spice of life in your choice of games

319
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
as in everything else.

320
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
So that's the first way to kill a scout troop-

321
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
is to do the same thing:

322
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:14,000
play the same game every week at your troop meetings.

323
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
The second way to kill a scout troop

324
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
is to always work on badges.

325
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
It's just like school.

326
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,000
Too great an emphasis on earning

327
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
those little bits of embroidered cloth

328
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
in a school-type environment doesn't go a long way

329
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
towards holding a scout's interest.

330
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:32,000
Instead, you should be trying to include activities

331
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:35,000
that will cover a badge's requirements almost automatically.

332
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
When scouts do the things scouts do, scouts advance.

333
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
We don't do things to advance,

334
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
We do things because that's what scouts do,

335
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
Right? So that's the second.

336
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
The third way to kill a scout troop:

337
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
we go to the same campsite every single month.

338
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
Go someplace different,

339
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,000
Right? If you repeat the same campsite,

340
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:59,000
try not to do the same one, maybe more than twice a year.

341
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
This way scouts will have a little bit of variety.

342
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
They may have their favorites

343
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
and you'll return to them at one point

344
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
during their scouting career.

345
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
You don't want your scout staying away

346
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:13,000
because they've been there and done that,

347
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Right.

348
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:17,000
Number four: we always have to do the same thing

349
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
as the new scouts do.

350
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
Now, older scouts need some separation

351
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
from the younger scouts, in part because

352
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
their social needs are changing.

353
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
The challenge is in providing a program

354
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
to fit the needs of two developmental groups

355
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:30,000
at the same time.

356
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
One way to do that is the older scouts

357
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
become more involved in the development

358
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
and operation of troop activities.

359
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
They should also receive more advanced leadership

360
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
and skills training.

361
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
Number five: all of our camps do the same.

362
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:45,000
No matter where we go, we do the same thing.

363
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
So you need to- you know,

364
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:50,000
have a varied menu of activities,

365
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
Not just a varied menu of places,

366
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
but a varied menu of activities.

367
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:57,000
Do you do the exact same thing every time you go camping.

368
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:00,000
You might want to turn its head every once in a while.

369
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
Our scouts like an occasional weekend

370
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:05,000
where they go on a camping trip that has no plan.

371
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
That's right. You find a campsite

372
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:08,000
on a good weekend,

373
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
They do their own thing

374
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:13,000
and they really enjoy doing this every once in a while.

375
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
Number six: we always meet in the same place,

376
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
and it's really boring.

377
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
Now you don't have to meet in the same place

378
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:21,000
week after week after week,

379
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
Perhaps one meeting a month.

380
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
you go somewhere else

381
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
from amongst the many different places

382
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
available to meet in your community

383
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:32,000
And you know what. That's a really great idea.

384
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
I gotta tell you that's a really great idea.

385
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
Not something I really thought of before.

386
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
but I'm gonna follow up on number six

387
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
And number seven. the last one:

388
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
we're supposed to run the troop, but the adults never let us.

389
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:43,000
Okay, folks,

390
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
Baden Powell said that the scouts

391
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
were supposed to manage the troop

392
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:50,000
and we really need to let them do it as advertised.

393
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
The thing is, we all know

394
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
kids the age of our scouts

395
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:57,000
can't really provide all the leadership that's needed, right?

396
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
That's not true.

397
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
Of course you need to train your scouts

398
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
to provide that kind of leadership. huh,

399
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Letting go of much of what you may perceive

400
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:09,000
as your leadership responsibility is hard to do,

401
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:12,000
especially if your troop has been adult-led for a long time.

402
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
But your troops best growth and strength

403
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:18,000
will come when you have a youth-led program

404
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
And these young people can really surprise you

405
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
with how well they can plan and run things

406
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:25,000
once they are properly trained.

407
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
Leadership training begins with a scout's

408
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
investiture into your troop.

409
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
It's a never-ending process

410
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
that neither you nor any scout

411
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
will ever complete while you're together.

412
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
Very well said:

413
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
Leadership, running their program

414
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:42,000
and leading their troop is a big subject

415
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
with us here at the Scoutmaster blog

416
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
and on scoutmastercgcom.

417
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
I just thought that was particularly well worded.

418
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:51,000
That's from Australia, from Scouts Australia.

419
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
Seven ways to kill a troop,

420
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
And of course it's intended

421
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
so that you'll learn about those pitfalls

422
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
and hopefully avoid them.

423
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
That's right. it's time

424
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:07,000
for a Scoutmasters Minute.

425
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:19,000
We see heroes in movies and we hear about them.

426
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:21,000
but what is a hero?

427
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
I think the best definition that I've been able to come up with

428
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:27,000
is that a hero is someone who faces danger and adversity,

429
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:30,000
usually from a position of weakness,

430
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
and they do it with courage

431
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:35,000
and with the idea that they're going to sacrifice themselves

432
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
for some greater good.

433
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
I started thinking about this

434
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
because, of course, this past weekend

435
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:44,000
we had the 10th anniversary

436
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:48,000
of the events of September 11th 2001..

437
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:52,000
Now four planes were hijacked by terrorists on that day.

438
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
Two crashed into the World Trade Center towers

439
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
in New York City.

440
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
One was flown into the Pentagon.

441
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:02,000
Flight 93 crashed into a field in western Pennsylvania

442
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
20 minutes from its intended target,

443
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
the Capitol building.

444
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
I don't think we've thought real hard

445
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
about what would have happened

446
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
if the people who were on Flight 93

447
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
didn't do what they did.

448
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
The passengers on Flight 93 made phone calls

449
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:19,000
from the plane after it was hijacked

450
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
and they were told that the three other planes

451
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:24,000
that morning had been flown into their targets.

452
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:27,000
They held together in the rear of the plane

453
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
and they voted.

454
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:32,000
They took a vote to attack the cockpit

455
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
and take control of the plane,

456
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:37,000
knowing full time

457
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:40,000
that they were probably sacrificing their lives.

458
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
Soon after they made that vote,

459
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
soon after they attacked the cockpit,

460
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:48,000
the plane crashed and the attack was thwarted.

461
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:52,000
December 10, 2011,. this past Saturday

462
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:55,000
was the dedication of the Flight 93 Memorial

463
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
in Shanksville, Pennsylvania.

464
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
Former President Bill Clinton delivered

465
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
a short but meaningful speech,

466
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
and this is what he said.

467
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:05,000
He said there's always been a special place

468
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,000
in the common memory for people

469
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,000
who deliberately, knowingly,

470
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
certainly, laid down their lives

471
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
so that other people could live.

472
00:27:13,000 --> 00:27:15,000
The President then went on to describe

473
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,000
how the defenders of the Alamo

474
00:27:17,000 --> 00:27:19,000
and the Spartans in the past

475
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:21,000
sacrifice their lives deliberately,

476
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,000
knowingly, and certainly.

477
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:26,000
In both instances, the casualties that they took

478
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
and the time they bought

479
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
saved the people that they loved.

480
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:33,000
The President then continued on and said:

481
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:36,000
your loved ones just happened to be on a plane,

482
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
With almost no time to decide.

483
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:41,000
they gave the entire country an incalculable gift.

484
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:44,000
They saved the capital from attack,

485
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
They saved God knows how many lives

486
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
and they saved the terrorists

487
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
from claiming the symbolic victory

488
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:53,000
of smashing the center of American government.

489
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
And they did it as citizens.

490
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
They allowed us to survive as a country

491
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
that could fight terror and still

492
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
maintain liberty

493
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
and still welcome people from all over the world,

494
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
from every religion, race and culture,

495
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
so long as they shared our values,

496
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:12,000
because ordinary people, given no time at all,

497
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
decided to do the right thing.

498
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:18,000
Now, the citizens on the plane that day:

499
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:21,000
they were men and women and mothers and fathers and children.

500
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:24,000
They were executives, technicians, students,

501
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:28,000
retirees, old, young, black, white

502
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:32,000
Americans and foreign-born visitors.

503
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
In a moment of time, they overcame

504
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
any apparent differences with courage,

505
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:40,000
bravery and selflessness

506
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:43,000
and actually achieved the first victory

507
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
in the war on terrorism.

508
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:48,000
Heroes are made when people do the right thing.

509
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
They do the right thing because

510
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
they're prepared mentally, physically and spiritually

511
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
to respond when the time comes.

512
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:57,000
The opportunity to respond as the passengers

513
00:28:57,000 --> 00:29:00,000
on that flight. that day may come to us,

514
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
but the opportunity to do the right thing

515
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
presents itself to us every day,

516
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:05,000
Every day,

517
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:08,000
Sometimes many times a day.

518
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
What we do as scouts challenges us

519
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:13,000
to be prepared to do the right thing.

520
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:16,000
in mind and body and heart.

521
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,000
We are prepared to respond to the everyday

522
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
opportunities of life

523
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
by knowing the right thing to do

524
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:26,000
and then possessing the courage to do it,

525
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:31,000
To be trustworthy, loyal, helpful,

526
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:35,000
friendly, courteous, kind, obedient,

527
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:39,000
cheerful, thrifty, brave, clean

528
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
and reverent.

